PERL BINDINGS FOR LIBAPREQ2
============================

The Perl bindings are included in the complete libapreq2 distribution.
This README is for developers modifying the Perl XS implementation.

    ./buildconf
    perl Makefile.PL
    make release

Run these commands from the repository root. Neither 'make' nor
'make test' is required to assemble the tarball.

PERL BUILD DEPENDENCIES
-----------------------

The Perl bindings require mod_perl2, Apache::Test,
ExtUtils::MakeMaker, and ExtUtils::XSBuilder. ExtUtils::XSBuilder must
be available to Perl when running the top-level buildconf script. The
remaining dependencies are required when configuring, building, or
testing the Perl bindings.

Dependencies installed into a local library using cpm are not
automatically added to Perl's module search path.

From the top-level distribution directory:

    cpm install -L local Apache::Test mod_perl2 ExtUtils::XSBuilder
    export PERL5LIB="$PWD/local/lib/perl5"

Verify that XSBuilder is accessible:

    perl -MExtUtils::XSBuilder -e \
        'print "$ExtUtils::XSBuilder::VERSION\n"'

This environment is also used when developing the XS bindings
directly from the glue/perl directory.

Creating a CPAN release does not require manually building or
testing the XS bindings. The top-level release process handles the
necessary source generation and packaging. See the top-level README
for release instructions.

The instructions below are a separate development workflow for working
directly on the Perl bindings; they are not release prerequisites.

DEVELOPING THE XS BINDINGS
-------------------------

The top-level build system uses ExtUtils::XSBuilder to generate Perl XS
sources and MakeMaker files from the libapreq headers. The Perl-specific
build also uses mod_perl2, Apache::Test, and ExtUtils::MakeMaker.

From glue/perl, to parse the libapreq headers into XSBuilder tables:

    perl ../../build/xsbuilder.pl run

To generate the xs/ directory and configure the Perl tests directly:

    perl Makefile.PL -apxs /path/to/apache2/bin/apxs

To run the Perl test suite from that directory after building:

    make
    make test

For the complete library, Apache module, and Perl bindings, use the
repository root instead:

    perl Makefile.PL --with-apache2-apxs=/path/to/apache2/bin/apxs
    make
    make test

APACHE TEST CONFIGURATION
-------------------------

The Apache::Test suites use isolated server configurations. They do not
necessarily inherit the modules enabled by the system's regular Apache
configuration.

During configuration, apxs supplies the Apache module directory. The
test templates conditionally load mod_mpm_prefork and mod_authz_core if
they are not already loaded. These settings apply to both the Apache
module tests and the Perl XS tests.

The generated templates are:

    module/t/conf/extra.conf.in
    glue/perl/t/conf/extra.conf.in

Their authoritative source templates are stored under build/templates/
at the repository root. Edit those source templates, not the generated
files in the test directories.

DEVELOPER NOTES

APR::Request::* should be a simple translation of the apreq C API:

apreq_handle_t -> APR::Request-derived instance.
apreq_module_t -> APR::Request-derived class.
apreq_param_t  -> APR::Request::Param
apreq_cookie_t -> APR::Request::Cookie
apreq_parser_t -> APR::Request::Parser (todo)
apreq_hook_t   -> APR::Request::Hook (todo)


--------------------------------------------------
Current APR:: module breakdown
--------------------------------------------------

APR::Request

  subs:
    encode
    decode

  methods:
    parse
    read_limit
    brigade_limit
    temp_dir
    header_in
    header_out
    jar_status
    args_status
    body_status
    param_status
    disable_uploads

  missing:
    add_hook (requires APR::Request::Hook)
    parser (requires APR::Request::Parser)

==================================================

APR::Request::CGI @ISA=(APR::Request)

  method:
    param

  ctor:
    new

==================================================

APR::Request::Apache2 @ISA=(APR::Request)

  method:
    param

  ctor:
    new

==================================================

APR::Request::Error  @ISA=(APR::Error, APR::Request)

  subs:
    strerror

==================================================

APR::Request::Cookie

  provides:
